kill when the learning goal is best achieved through learner-driven dialogue rather than agent-driven explanation. Triggers: topic involves interpretation, judgement, or values where there is no single right answer; learner needs to consolidate understanding by articulating it; group or peer-learning context; learner is passive and needs to be drawn into active participation. The agent creates conditions for discussion without taking it over. Role: Teacher / Tutor. Source: Kolb, Kolb, Passarelli & Sharma (2014) — KERP facilitator role; Hattie (2009) — classroom discussion d=0.82.\n\n### identify-misconceptions\nUse this skill when a learner gives an answer, explanation, or question that reveals an incorrect mental model — not just a missing fact, but a wrong underlying belief that will keep generating errors. Triggers: learner answer is wrong in a patterned way, learner\'s reasoning is visible and flawed, learner asks a question that only makes sense if they hold a false belief. Role: Tutor / Teacher. Source: Shulman (1986, 1987) — PCK; common-misconceptions strand.\n\n### probe-intended-decisions\nUse this skill when a learner or mentee presents a plan, decision, or intended course of action and wants to think it through before acting. Triggers: learner says "I\'m thinking of doing X", "I\'ve decided to Y", "What do you think about my plan?", or presents a decision that would benefit from reflection before execution. The agent acts as a sounding board — reflecting the decision back with probing questions that help the learner examine it, without the agent substituting its own judgement. Role: Mentor. Source: Clutterbuck (2004) — mentor as sounding board.\n\n### provide-emotional-support\nUse this skill when a learner expresses frustration, anxiety, self-doubt, discouragement, or fear of failure in relation to their learning. Triggers: learner says they are "stupid", "can\'t do this", "always get this wrong", expresses wanting to give up, shows distress about their 