edges the emotional state, normalises struggle, and maintains psychological safety — without replacing appropriate challenge. Role: Mentor / Tutor. Source: Kram (1983, 1985) — psychosocial mentor functions: counselling and acceptance.\n\n### provide-formative-feedback\nUse this skill whenever a learner submits work, answers a question, or completes a task and the goal is to improve their learning — not merely grade or judge. Triggers: learner submits an answer, draft, explanation, or attempt at any task during active instruction. This skill selects the right level of feedback (task, process, or self-regulation) and delivers it in a form the learner can act on immediately. Role: Tutor / Teacher. Source: Hattie (2009) Visible Learning (feedback effect size d=0.73); Hattie & Timperley (2007) The Power of Feedback.\n\n### reflect-on-pedagogy\nUse this skill after any instructional interaction — tutoring session, lesson delivery, or feedback exchange — to evaluate whether the pedagogical decisions made were effective and identify specific improvements for the next interaction. Triggers: end of a session, learner showed signs of persistent confusion despite instruction, an explanation did not land, or an assessment revealed unexpected gaps. The agent reviews its own teaching decisions and produces a structured reflection. Role: Educator. Source: Shulman (1987) — pedagogical reasoning cycle: reflection and new comprehension steps; Hattie (2009) — teachers as evaluators of their own teaching.\n\n### scaffold-and-release\nUse this skill when a learner cannot yet complete a task independently but could complete it with structured support. Triggers: learner is stuck, makes repeated errors on the same step, asks for help, or produces work that is almost-right-but-not-quite. The skill provides the minimum support needed, then tracks mastery and removes support as the learner gains competence. Role: Tutor. Source: Vygotsky (1978) — Zone of Proximal Development; Wood, Bruner & Ross (1976) — scaffolding theory.\n\n### set-learning-objectives\nUse this skill at the start of any instructional interaction — lesson, tutoring session, or explanation — to establish what the learner will be able to do by the end and how both the agent and learner will know success has been achieved. Triggers: start of a new topic, new session, or when the learner or agent has no shared understanding of what the goal of the interaction is. Role: Teacher / Educator. Source: Hattie (2009) Visible Learning — teacher clarity effect size d=0.75.\n\n### transform-content\nUse this skill when an educational agent must explain a concept to a learner whose background, level, or misconceptions differ from a standard presentation of the topic. Triggers: learner says "I don\'t understand", gives a wrong answer, asks for a simpler explanation, or the agent detects a mismatch between the concept\'s canonical form and what the learner can currently access. Role: Tutor / Teacher. Source: Shulman (1986, 1987) — Pedagogical Content Knowledge (PCK).\n\n\n## NOW WRITE YOUR TURN\nReply with EXACTLY these five header lines and then your message to the student. Do NOT repeat or list the skills above — choose ONE and name it on the SKILL: line. Do NOT output any headings, bullet lists, or notes from these instructions.\n\nSKILL: <one skill name from the list above>\nREASONING: <one short sentence, for the log — the student never sees it>\nSTAGE: <the stage number you are acting in>\nSTAGE_COMPLETE: <YES or NO>\nTASK_SOLVED: <YES or NO>\n<your message to the student — ONE question, nothing else>\n\n\n## ⚠ YOUR LAST ATTEMPT WAS INVALID\nYou listed the skills instead of speaking to the student. The header block is already written for you. Write ONLY your message to the student now — one short question, no headings, no bullet lists, no skill names.<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nCould you tell me about your research question?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nwell i am looking to explore teachers perceptions towards sustainability education practices<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nWhat method are you thinking of using — and if you\'re not sure yet, that\'s completely fine?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\ni am going to use interviews<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nThat\'s a good start. Interviews can be a rich source of data. What programme are you on — Masters or PhD, full-time or part-time — and what year are you in?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nMasters part time in my first year<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nWould you be more comfortable using qualitative methods, or are you open to exploring quantitative methods as well?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nopen to anything open to anything at this stage<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nYou mentioned you\'re open to anything, but I\'m guessing you might have some reservations or preferences about qualitative versus quantitative methods. What do you think might be the advantages or disadvantages of using each approach for your research on teachers\' perceptions of sustainability education practices?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nwell qualitative would take lots of time but could be richer quantiative could be quicker but less meaninful<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nCan you tell me a bit about your research project?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nwell i just told you<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nYou mentioned earlier that you\'re looking to explore teachers\' perceptions towards sustainability education practices. Can you describe why you feel more confident using qualitative methods, despite the potential time commitment, and what you hope to gain from a richer, more in-depth understanding of teachers\' perceptions?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nwell i like talkting to peoplee<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nThinking about your research question, is your aim more exploratory, explanatory, or evaluative?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nwhat do they mean<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nLet me break it down for you. Exploratory research is like taking a first look at something - you\'re trying to understand what\'s out there, what people think, and what\'s happening. Explanatory research is like trying to figure out why something is happening - you\'re looking for causes and effects. Evaluative research is like judging something - you\'re trying to decide if something is good or bad, effective or not. Which of these sounds closest to what you\'re trying to do with your research on teachers\' perceptions of sustainability education practices?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\ni think i would likee to evaluate if they are open to sustainability practices in their classroom and why<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nThinking about your research question, what type of information or data do you think you would need to answer it?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nmaybe words?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nYes, words could be a good starting point. You might consider using qualitative methods like interviews or focus groups to gather teachers\' thoughts and opinions about sustainability practices. Alternatively, you could use quantitative methods like surveys to collect numerical data on teachers\' attitudes and behaviors. Or, you might consider a mixed-methods approach that combines both qualitative and quantitative data. What do you think about these different options?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nit feels a bit overwhelming<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nDon\'t worry, it\'s normal to feel overwhelmed when considering different research methods. Let\'s take a step back and think about what you\'re trying to achieve with your research. You want to evaluate if teachers are open to sustainability practices in their classroom and why. What type of evidence would you find most convincing: measurable or observable data (a positivist perspective), people\'s experiences and meanings (an interpretivist perspective), or whatever best helps answer the research question (a pragmatic perspective)?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\ncan you tell me a bit more about the interpretivist perspective<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nAn interpretivist perspective assumes that people create their own meanings and interpretations of the world around them. In your research, this might mean exploring how teachers make sense of sustainability practices and what they think they mean for their classrooms. This approach often involves collecting and analyzing qualitative data, like interview transcripts or observational notes, to understand people\'s experiences and perspectives. Does that sound like it might be a good fit for your research question?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\ncan you tell me more about the positivest perspectiv<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nA positivist perspective assumes that there\'s an objective reality that can be measured and observed. In your research, this might mean using surveys or questionnaires to collect numerical data on teachers\' attitudes and behaviors towards sustainability practices. This approach often involves using statistical analysis to identify patterns and trends in the data. Would a positivist approach be feasible for your research, given your timeline and resources?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\ni think the positivist perpective mightbe best<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nThat\'s a good choice. A positivist approach can provide a clear and objective understanding of teachers\' attitudes and behaviors towards sustainability practices. Now, let\'s consider a specific research method that aligns with this perspective. Have you considered using a survey to collect data from teachers?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nyes but are there any other ways<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nBesides surveys, another method that might work for a positivist approach is a content analysis of existing documents, such as school policies or curriculum plans, to see how sustainability is represented. Would that be something you\'d be interested in exploring?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\ngive m 6 other ideas<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nHere are six other research method ideas that might work for your study:\n\n1. Observational study: You could observe teachers\' classrooms to see how they incorporate sustainability practices into their teaching.\n2. Experimental design: You could design an experiment to test the effect of a specific sustainability practice on student outcomes.\n3. Case study: You could conduct an in-depth study of a single school or classroom to explore how they implement sustainability practices.\n4. Document analysis: You could analyze existing documents, such as school reports or curriculum plans, to see how sustainability is represented.\n5. Social network analysis: You could study the relationships between teachers, administrators, and other stakeholders to see how they influence sustainability practices.\n6. Archival research: You could analyze existing data, such as student test scores or school records, to see how sustainability practices have impacted student outcomes over time.\n\nWhich of these methods do you think might be a good fit for your research question?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\ngive me 10 more<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nHere are 10 more research method ideas that might work for your study:\n\n1. Comparative study: You could compare the sustainability practices of different schools or classrooms to identify best practices.\n2. Longitudinal study: You could conduct a long-term study to see how sustainability practices change over time.\n3. Mixed-methods study: You could combine qualitative and quantitative methods to get a more comprehensive understanding of sustainability practices.\n4. Phenomenological study: You could explore the lived experiences of teachers and students to understand how they perceive sustainability practices.\n5. Grounded theory study: You could develop a theory about sustainability practices based on data collected from teachers and students.\n6. Discourse analysis: You could analyze the language and communication patterns used by teachers and students to discuss sustainability practices.\n7. Ethnographic study: You could conduct a detailed study of a single school or classroom to understand the cultural context of sustainability practices.\n8. Historical study: You could examine the development of sustainability practices over time to identify key trends and patterns.\n9. Multilevel study: You could examine the impact of sustainability practices at multiple levels, such as individual, classroom, and school.\n10. Participatory action research: You could work with teachers and students to design and implement sustainability practices and study their impact.\n\nWhich of these methods do you think might be a good fit for your research question?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nare these all positivist<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nNo, not all of the methods I listed are positivist. Some, such as phenomenological study, grounded theory study, and discourse analysis, are more aligned with interpretivist or constructivist perspectives. Others, such as comparative study, longitudinal study, and multilevel study, could be used with either positivist or interpretivist approaches, depending on the research question and design. Would you like me to help you identify which methods are most closely aligned with a positivist perspective?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nyes please<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nSKILL: scaffold-and-release\nREASONING: The student needs to consider alternative methods to broaden their awareness and reflect on their feasibility.\nSTAGE: 4\nSTAGE_COMPLETE: NO\nTASK_SOLVED: NO\nRESPONSE:', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.2, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=['<|im_end|>', '<|im_start|>', '<|eot_id|>', '<|start_header_id|>', '<|end_header_id|>', '<|endoftext|>', '\nUser:', '\nUSER:', '\nStudent:', '\nSTUDENT', '\nHuman:', '\nMentor:', '\nMENTOR', '\nAoife:', '\nAssistant:', '\nASSISTANT:', '\nTeacher:', '\nSupervisor:', '\n## Available skills', '\nAvailable skills:'], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=300, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: None, lora_request: None, prompt_adapter_request: None.
